#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# multiplicative_order

#% text
# `multiplicative_order(a, n)` gives the least `k >= 1` with `mod(a^k, n) == 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The order exists when `gcd(a, n) == 1`; otherwise the call stays unevaluated. It divides `carmichael_lambda(n)` and `euler_phi(n)`.

#% item
# `multiplicative_order(a, n, [r1, r2, ...])` gives the least `k >= 1` with `a^k` congruent to one of the `r_i` modulo `n` (a single residue may be given without the list); it stays unevaluated when no power of `a` reaches them. A list for `a` or `n` threads.

#% item
# `multiplicative_order(a)` of a nonzero element `a` of a finite field is the least `k >= 1` with `a^k == 1`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
multiplicative_order(2, 7)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
multiplicative_order(10, 7)
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
multiplicative_order(3, 100)
#% output
#= 20

#% text
# The least power of 5 that is congruent to 3 or 11 modulo 7, and the least power of 2 that is congruent to 1 or -1 modulo 15:

#% cell
multiplicative_order(5, 7, [3, 11])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
multiplicative_order(2, 15, [1, -1])
#% output
#= 4

#% text
# The order of an element of a finite field (the field of 9 elements has 8 nonzero ones):

#% cell
multiplicative_order(finite_field(3, 2)([1, 1]))
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The period of the decimal expansion of `1/7` is the order of 10 modulo 7:

#% cell
real_digits(1/7)
#% output
#= [[[1, 4, 2, 8, 5, 7]], 0]

#% text
# Periods of `1/p` for some primes:

#% cell
table(multiplicative_order(10, p), p, [3, 7, 11, 13, 17])
#% output
#= [1, 6, 2, 6, 16]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The order divides `carmichael_lambda(n)`:

#% cell
mod(carmichael_lambda(100), multiplicative_order(3, 100))
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `primitive_root`, `carmichael_lambda`, `power_mod`, `euler_phi`
